/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Isaiah chapters 61-65
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("isaiah", [
{n:61, name:"Beauty for Ashes", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 61:1",verse:"The Spirit of the Lord GOD is upon me; because the LORD hath anointed me to preach good tidings unto the meek; he hath sent me to bind up the brokenhearted, to proclaim liberty to the captives, and the opening of the prison to them that are bound;"}, facts:["Did you know Jesus read Isaiah 61 in the synagogue at Nazareth and said, \"This day is this scripture fulfilled in your ears\" (Luke 4)? He stopped mid verse, before the day of vengeance, because the day of grace had come first.","Did you know the name Hephzi-bah and Beulah in the next chapter get their stage set here? First Zion mourns in ashes; then she is renamed. Restoration in Isaiah always follows repentance, never the other way round.","Did you know the priests of Israel came only from the tribe of Levi, yet Isaiah 61 promises a whole people named \"Priests of the LORD\"? That is why Peter later calls the whole church a royal priesthood (1 Peter 2)."], questions:[
{q:"Upon whom does the Spirit of the Lord GOD rest in Isaiah 61:1?", options:["Upon the speaker, because the LORD has anointed him to preach good tidings","Upon the kings of Judah because of their wisdom","Upon the watchmen of Jerusalem to keep them awake","Upon the priests because of their sacrifices"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 61:1",verse:"The Spirit of the Lord GOD is upon me; because the LORD hath anointed me to preach good tidings unto the meek; he hath sent me to bind up the brokenhearted, to proclaim liberty to the captives, and the opening of the prison to them that are bound;", insight:"Anointing in Scripture is always for a task, not a title. The Spirit comes so that good news is preached, the brokenhearted are bound up, and captives are freed."},
{q:"According to Isaiah 61:1, to whom is liberty proclaimed?", options:["To the captives, and the opening of the prison to them that are bound","To the merchants of Jerusalem","To the watchmen on the walls","To the sons of the alien"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 61:1",verse:"The Spirit of the Lord GOD is upon me; because the LORD hath anointed me to preach good tidings unto the meek; he hath sent me to bind up the brokenhearted, to proclaim liberty to the captives, and the opening of the prison to them that are bound;", insight:"The gospel is first a rescue announcement. Before it is advice for the good, it is freedom for those who cannot free themselves."},
{q:"What two things are proclaimed together in Isaiah 61:2?", options:["The acceptable year of the LORD, and the day of vengeance of our God","The building of the temple, and the keeping of the Sabbath","The defeat of Edom, and the wealth of the nations","The famine of the land, and the year of jubilee"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 61:2",verse:"To proclaim the acceptable year of the LORD, and the day of vengeance of our God; to comfort all that mourn;", insight:"Grace and judgment are both real. The acceptable year comes first so that every mourner has a door of mercy open before the day of vengeance arrives."},
{q:"Whom is the anointed one sent to comfort in Isaiah 61:2?", options:["All that mourn","All that laugh","All that trade","All that build"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 61:2",verse:"To proclaim the acceptable year of the LORD, and the day of vengeance of our God; to comfort all that mourn;", insight:"God's comfort is not reserved for the deserving. The invitation is wide on purpose: all who mourn are named."},
{q:"What is given to those who mourn in Zion in place of ashes?", options:["Beauty","Strength","Wisdom","Riches"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 61:3",verse:"To appoint unto them that mourn in Zion, to give unto them beauty for ashes, the oil of joy for mourning, the garment of praise for the spirit of heaviness; that they might be called trees of righteousness, the planting of the LORD, that he might be glorified.", insight:"God trades with mourners, not exploits them. Ashes, the sign of grief, are exchanged for beauty, which means sorrow has no final word in Zion."},
{q:"In Isaiah 61:5, what shall strangers do?", options:["Stand and feed your flocks","Build the city walls","Keep the temple gates","Carry the king's armor"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 61:5",verse:"And strangers shall stand and feed your flocks, and the sons of the alien shall be your plowmen and your vinedressers.", insight:"God lets outsiders share in the work of restoration. No one is too foreign for his household when he decides to rebuild."},
{q:"Those who mourn in Zion will be called what, according to Isaiah 61:3?", options:["Trees of righteousness, the planting of the LORD","Sons of the desert, the children of the wind","Keepers of the gate, the pillars of the wall","Servants of the law, the singers of Zion"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 61:3",verse:"To appoint unto them that mourn in Zion, to give unto them beauty for ashes, the oil of joy for mourning, the garment of praise for the spirit of heaviness; that they might be called trees of righteousness, the planting of the LORD, that he might be glorified.", insight:"God renames his wounded ones by what he is growing in them. A tree of righteousness is not praised for its past but planted for its future fruit."},
{q:"What will the restored people build, according to Isaiah 61:4?", options:["The old wastes and the former desolations","A new temple of cedar and gold","A wall around Jerusalem","High towers for the watchmen"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 61:4",verse:"And they shall build the old wastes, they shall raise up the former desolations, and they shall repair the waste cities, the desolations of many generations.", insight:"God does not only comfort ruins; he puts his people to work on them. Healing that never rebuilds is incomplete."},
{q:"The waste cities they repair are described as the desolations of what?", options:["Many generations","One bitter year","The recent war","A single lifetime"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 61:4",verse:"And they shall build the old wastes, they shall raise up the former desolations, and they shall repair the waste cities, the desolations of many generations.", insight:"Some brokenness is older than you. God gives grace that outlasts the ruins, restoring what many generations watched fall."},
{q:"In Isaiah 61:5, who will be the plowmen and vinedressers?", options:["The sons of the alien","The sons of Judah","The Levites of the temple","The elders of Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 61:5",verse:"And strangers shall stand and feed your flocks, and the sons of the alien shall be your plowmen and your vinedressers.", insight:"Restoration reverses roles. Those once outside now serve within God's household, and the old divisions give way to a new economy of grace."},
{q:"What shall Israel be named in Isaiah 61:6?", options:["The Priests of the LORD","The Keepers of the Law","The Sons of the Desert","The Builders of the Wall"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 61:6",verse:"But ye shall be named the Priests of the LORD: men shall call you the Ministers of our God: ye shall eat the riches of the Gentiles, and in their glory shall ye boast yourselves.", insight:"Every believer becomes a priest, someone with direct access to God and a ministry to others. Holiness is no longer a tribal specialty but a people's calling."},
{q:"In Isaiah 61:6, in what shall the people boast?", options:["In their glory, eating the riches of the Gentiles","In the strength of their armies","In the height of their walls","In the number of their flocks"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 61:6",verse:"But ye shall be named the Priests of the LORD: men shall call you the Ministers of our God: ye shall eat the riches of the Gentiles, and in their glory shall ye boast yourselves.", insight:"True boasting is in what God has done, not in what we have earned. The riches here are the nations' tribute to God's grace, not plunder we seized."},
{q:"For your shame you shall have what, according to Isaiah 61:7?", options:["Double","Mercy","Silence","Rest"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 61:7",verse:"For your shame ye shall have double; and for confusion they shall rejoice in their portion: therefore in their land they shall possess the double: everlasting joy shall be unto them.", insight:"God's arithmetic with shame is multiplication, not subtraction. Where there was double disgrace, he answers with double portion."},
{q:"What shall be unto the restored people forever in Isaiah 61:7?", options:["Everlasting joy","Everlasting silence","Everlasting labor","Everlasting debt"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 61:7",verse:"For your shame ye shall have double; and for confusion they shall rejoice in their portion: therefore in their land they shall possess the double: everlasting joy shall be unto them.", insight:"Joy in God's restoration is not a mood but a possession. It is given as an inheritance that outlives the circumstances that threatened it."},
{q:"What does the LORD say he hates in Isaiah 61:8?", options:["Robbery for burnt offering","The songs of the temple","The prayers of the priests","The wealth of the nations"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 61:8",verse:"For I the LORD love judgment, I hate robbery for burnt offering; and I will direct their work in truth, and I will make an everlasting covenant with them.", insight:"God will not accept worship paid for with injustice. A gift to God that was stolen from your neighbor is not generosity; it is robbery dressed up as devotion."},
{q:"What will the LORD make with his people in Isaiah 61:8?", options:["An everlasting covenant","A temporary truce","A written contract","A spoken promise"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 61:8",verse:"For I the LORD love judgment, I hate robbery for burnt offering; and I will direct their work in truth, and I will make an everlasting covenant with them.", insight:"God's commitments do not expire. An everlasting covenant means his people can plan their future on his faithfulness, not their performance."},
{q:"In Isaiah 61:9, what will all who see them acknowledge?", options:["That they are the seed which the LORD hath blessed","That they are the builders of great cities","That they are the sons of mighty kings","That they are the keepers of ancient law"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 61:9",verse:"And their seed shall be known among the Gentiles, and their offspring among the people: all that see them shall acknowledge them, that they are the seed which the LORD hath blessed.", insight:"Blessing is meant to be visible. A life shaped by God becomes evidence that strangers can read, even those who do not share the faith."},
{q:"With what has God clothed the speaker in Isaiah 61:10?", options:["The garments of salvation","The armor of battle","The robes of the priests","The cloak of the prophets"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 61:10",verse:"I will greatly rejoice in the LORD, my soul shall be joyful in my God; for he hath clothed me with the garments of salvation, he hath covered me with the robe of righteousness, as a bridegroom decketh himself with ornaments, and as a bride adorneth herself with her jewels.", insight:"Salvation is something you wear, given to you and covering you. We do not tailor our own righteousness; God clothes us in his."},
{q:"In Isaiah 61:10, the speaker is decked with ornaments like whom?", options:["A bridegroom, and adorned with jewels as a bride","A king, and crowned as a conqueror","A shepherd, and staffed as a guardian","A scribe, and robed as a teacher"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 61:10",verse:"I will greatly rejoice in the LORD, my soul shall be joyful in my God; for he hath clothed me with the garments of salvation, he hath covered me with the robe of righteousness, as a bridegroom decketh himself with ornaments, and as a bride adorneth herself with her jewels.", insight:"God dresses his people for a wedding, not a courtroom. Salvation ends in celebration, and joy is the proper uniform of the redeemed."},
{q:"Before whom will the Lord GOD cause righteousness and praise to spring forth?", options:["Before all the nations","Before the priests alone","Before the kings of Judah","Before the elders of Israel"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 61:11",verse:"For as the earth bringeth forth her bud, and as the garden causeth the things that are sown in it to spring forth; so the Lord GOD will cause righteousness and praise to spring forth before all the nations.", insight:"God's work in his people is not private. Like a garden, it is meant to be seen and to draw the watching nations toward him."}
]},
{n:62, name:"The Bride's New Name", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 62:4",verse:"Thou shalt no more be termed Forsaken; neither shall thy land any more be termed Desolate: but thou shalt be called Hephzi-bah, and thy land Beulah: for the LORD delighteth in thee, and thy land shall be married."}, facts:["Did you know Hephzi-bah means \"my delight is in her\" and Beulah means \"married\"? God renames both the city and the land, turning labels of shame into declarations of love.","Did you know the watchmen of Isaiah 62 are told to \"give him no rest\"? Persistent prayer is pictured as refusing to let God rest until he keeps his promise, which is boldness born of trust, not irreverence.","Did you know Isaiah 62:11 echoes Zechariah 9:9, and both were fulfilled when Jesus rode into Jerusalem? \"Behold, thy salvation cometh\" was the crowd's hope on Palm Sunday."], questions:[
{q:"For whose sake will the prophet not hold his peace in Isaiah 62:1?", options:["For Zion's sake, and for Jerusalem's sake","For Egypt's sake, and for Assyria's sake","For Edom's sake, and for Moab's sake","For Tyre's sake, and for Sidon's sake"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 62:1",verse:"For Zion’s sake will I not hold my peace, and for Jerusalem’s sake I will not rest, until the righteousness thereof go forth as brightness, and the salvation thereof as a lamp that burneth.", insight:"Intercession refuses silence. Love for God's people means speaking up for them until righteousness breaks through like the dawn."},
{q:"In Isaiah 62:1, Jerusalem's salvation shall go forth as what?", options:["As a lamp that burneth","As a sword that flashes","As a trumpet that sounds","As a river that floods"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 62:1",verse:"For Zion’s sake will I not hold my peace, and for Jerusalem’s sake I will not rest, until the righteousness thereof go forth as brightness, and the salvation thereof as a lamp that burneth.", insight:"Salvation is not a secret kept in a drawer. God means it to shine, bright enough for a dark world to find its way by."},
{q:"Who shall see Jerusalem's righteousness, according to Isaiah 62:2?", options:["The Gentiles, and all kings her glory","Only the priests of the temple","Only the elders of Judah","Only the prophets of Israel"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 62:2",verse:"And the Gentiles shall see thy righteousness, and all kings thy glory: and thou shalt be called by a new name, which the mouth of the LORD shall name.", insight:"God's grace on one people is never meant to stop with them. The nations are invited to look and see what God does for his own."},
{q:"By what name shall Jerusalem be called in Isaiah 62:2?", options:["A new name, which the mouth of the LORD shall name","The old name her fathers gave her","The name the nations choose for her","A secret name no one may speak"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 62:2",verse:"And the Gentiles shall see thy righteousness, and all kings thy glory: and thou shalt be called by a new name, which the mouth of the LORD shall name.", insight:"When God renames you, your history no longer defines you. The new name comes from his mouth, which means only he has the right to say who you are."},
{q:"What shall Jerusalem be in the hand of the LORD in Isaiah 62:3?", options:["A crown of glory","A sword of judgment","A shield of bronze","A staff of iron"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 62:3",verse:"Thou shalt also be a crown of glory in the hand of the LORD, and a royal diadem in the hand of thy God.", insight:"God does not merely tolerate his people; he treasures them. To be a crown in his hand is to be the visible sign of his own honor."},
{q:"What shall she be in the hand of her God in Isaiah 62:3?", options:["A royal diadem","A golden cup","A silver trumpet","A woven cord"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 62:3",verse:"Thou shalt also be a crown of glory in the hand of the LORD, and a royal diadem in the hand of thy God.", insight:"A diadem is worn where all can see. God's people are not hidden in his pocket but displayed as the mark of his royalty."},
{q:"In Isaiah 62:4, she shall no more be termed what?", options:["Forsaken","Forgotten","Defeated","Silent"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 62:4",verse:"Thou shalt no more be termed Forsaken; neither shall thy land any more be termed Desolate: but thou shalt be called Hephzi-bah, and thy land Beulah: for the LORD delighteth in thee, and thy land shall be married.", insight:"Forsaken is a word the abandoned believe about themselves. God deletes it from their name, because his presence makes it a lie."},
{q:"What shall Jerusalem's land be called in Isaiah 62:4?", options:["Beulah, for the LORD delighteth in thee, and thy land shall be married","Desolate, for the LORD has forgotten thee","Empty, for the strangers have taken it","Barren, for the rains have ceased"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 62:4",verse:"Thou shalt no more be termed Forsaken; neither shall thy land any more be termed Desolate: but thou shalt be called Hephzi-bah, and thy land Beulah: for the LORD delighteth in thee, and thy land shall be married.", insight:"Beulah means married, and marriage means belonging. God binds himself to his people with a covenant love that ends their loneliness."},
{q:"According to Isaiah 62:5, as a young man marries a virgin, so shall what happen?", options:["Thy sons shall marry thee","Thy enemies shall serve thee","Thy walls shall rise again","Thy fields shall yield again"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 62:5",verse:"For as a young man marrieth a virgin, so shall thy sons marry thee: and as the bridegroom rejoiceth over the bride, so shall thy God rejoice over thee.", insight:"God pictures restoration as a homecoming. The children return to their mother city with the joy of a bridegroom coming home to his bride."},
{q:"In Isaiah 62:5, as the bridegroom rejoices over the bride, so shall who rejoice over Jerusalem?", options:["Thy God","Thy king","Thy watchmen","Thy builders"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 62:5",verse:"For as a young man marrieth a virgin, so shall thy sons marry thee: and as the bridegroom rejoiceth over the bride, so shall thy God rejoice over thee.", insight:"God's joy over his people is not duty but delight. He rejoices the way a bridegroom does, with a happiness that cannot be hidden."},
{q:"Who has God set upon Jerusalem's walls in Isaiah 62:6?", options:["Watchmen, which shall never hold their peace day nor night","Archers, to shoot at the enemy","Builders, to repair the stones","Scribes, to record the law"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 62:6",verse:"I have set watchmen upon thy walls, O Jerusalem, which shall never hold their peace day nor night: ye that make mention of the LORD, keep not silence,", insight:"Watchmen are intercessors stationed in prayer. Their post is not a place of panic but of persistent, day and night pleading for the city."},
{q:"What are those who make mention of the LORD told to do in Isaiah 62:6?", options:["Keep not silence","Keep their distance","Keep the Sabbath","Keep the gates"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 62:6",verse:"I have set watchmen upon thy walls, O Jerusalem, which shall never hold their peace day nor night: ye that make mention of the LORD, keep not silence,", insight:"Reminding God of his promises is an act of faith, not forgetfulness on his part. Prayer keeps the promise alive in the heart of the one praying."},
{q:"Until what must they give the LORD no rest in Isaiah 62:7?", options:["Till he make Jerusalem a praise in the earth","Till he send rain upon the land","Till he defeat the nations","Till he rebuild the temple"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 62:7",verse:"And give him no rest, till he establish, and till he make Jerusalem a praise in the earth.", insight:"Holy stubbornness in prayer is commended here. We are told to press God's promises until the answer is established in the earth."},
{q:"By what has the LORD sworn in Isaiah 62:8?", options:["By his right hand, and by the arm of his strength","By the stars of heaven","By the throne of David","By the covenant of Noah"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 62:8",verse:"The LORD hath sworn by his right hand, and by the arm of his strength, Surely I will no more give thy corn to be meat for thine enemies; and the sons of the stranger shall not drink thy wine, for the which thou hast laboured:", insight:"When God wants you to be sure, he swears by himself. His right hand is his power and his arm is his action, both pledged to keep this word."},
{q:"What will the LORD no more give to her enemies as meat, according to Isaiah 62:8?", options:["Her corn","Her cattle","Her wine","Her oil"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 62:8",verse:"The LORD hath sworn by his right hand, and by the arm of his strength, Surely I will no more give thy corn to be meat for thine enemies; and the sons of the stranger shall not drink thy wine, for the which thou hast laboured:", insight:"God ends the economy of plunder. His people will no longer labor for others to enjoy; their harvest will stay in their own hands."},
{q:"Who shall eat the corn in Isaiah 62:9?", options:["They that have gathered it, praising the LORD","The strangers from afar","The kings of the earth","The armies of Assyria"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 62:9",verse:"But they that have gathered it shall eat it, and praise the LORD; and they that have brought it together shall drink it in the courts of my holiness.", insight:"God restores the link between labor and enjoyment. Work done in his ways ends in thanksgiving, not in watching others eat what you sowed."},
{q:"Where shall they drink the wine they have brought together?", options:["In the courts of my holiness","In the houses of the merchants","In the fields of the valley","In the gates of the city"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 62:9",verse:"But they that have gathered it shall eat it, and praise the LORD; and they that have brought it together shall drink it in the courts of my holiness.", insight:"The harvest becomes worship. Ordinary wine, gathered by ordinary hands, is drunk in God's courts, which means daily work can end at his table."},
{q:"What must be cast up and cleared of stones in Isaiah 62:10?", options:["The highways, and a standard lifted up for the people","The walls, and the gates repaired","The temple courts, and the altars cleansed","The vineyards, and the hedges mended"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 62:10",verse:"Go through, go through the gates; prepare ye the way of the people; cast up, cast up the highways; gather out the stones; lift up a standard for the people.", insight:"God's people prepare the way for others. Clearing stones from the road is ministry: removing obstacles so that people can come to him."},
{q:"What proclamation goes to the daughter of Zion in Isaiah 62:11?", options:["Behold, thy salvation cometh; behold, his reward is with him","Behold, thy king is fallen; behold, thy walls are broken","Behold, thy enemies approach; behold, thy gates are shut","Behold, thy harvest fails; behold, thy wells are dry"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 62:11",verse:"Behold, the LORD hath proclaimed unto the end of the world, Say ye to the daughter of Zion, Behold, thy salvation cometh; behold, his reward is with him, and his work before him.", insight:"Salvation comes to a person, not just as an idea. The proclamation is behold, because God's rescue arrives in a form you can see."},
{q:"What shall they be called in Isaiah 62:12?", options:["The holy people, The redeemed of the LORD; and thou, Sought out, A city not forsaken","The mighty people, The conquerors of the nations; and thou, Fortified, A city none can enter","The wealthy people, The traders of the sea; and thou, Enriched, A city full of gold","The silent people, The keepers of the law; and thou, Hidden, A city none can find"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 62:12",verse:"And they shall call them, The holy people, The redeemed of the LORD: and thou shalt be called, Sought out, A city not forsaken.", insight:"Redemption rewrites identity from the outside in. Sought out and not forsaken are the final words, which means God's last verdict on his people is love."}
]},
{n:63, name:"The Angel of His Presence", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 63:9",verse:"In all their affliction he was afflicted, and the angel of his presence saved them: in his love and in his pity he redeemed them; and he bare them, and carried them all the days of old."}, facts:["Did you know Edom, named in the opening verse, was Israel's ancient enemy descended from Esau? The prophet uses Edom's judgment as a picture of God's final judgment on all who oppose him.","Did you know the winepress image of Isaiah 63 appears again in Revelation 19, where Christ treads \"the winepress of the fierceness and wrath of Almighty God\"? Isaiah's vision of judgment points to a real future day.","Did you know Isaiah 63:10 says they \"vexed his holy Spirit\"? That is one of the clearest Old Testament hints that the Holy Spirit is a person who can be grieved, not just a force."], questions:[
{q:"From where does the one with dyed garments come in Isaiah 63:1?", options:["From Edom","From Egypt","From Assyria","From Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 63:1",verse:"Who is this that cometh from Edom, with dyed garments from Bozrah? this that is glorious in his apparel, travelling in the greatness of his strength? I that speak in righteousness, mighty to save.", insight:"Edom represents settled, proud opposition to God. Judgment begins where rebellion was longest nursed, which warns against presuming on old grudges."},
{q:"The dyed garments come from which city in Isaiah 63:1?", options:["Bozrah","Damascus","Nineveh","Tyre"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 63:1",verse:"Who is this that cometh from Edom, with dyed garments from Bozrah? this that is glorious in his apparel, travelling in the greatness of his strength? I that speak in righteousness, mighty to save.", insight:"God names real places in his judgments. Bozrah was a real city, which reminds us that prophecy speaks to history, not to vague abstractions."},
{q:"In Isaiah 63:2, his garments look like those of whom?", options:["Him that treadeth in the winefat","Him that ploweth the field","Him that keepeth the vineyard","Him that grindeth the corn"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 63:2",verse:"Wherefore art thou red in thine apparel, and thy garments like him that treadeth in the winefat?", insight:"The winefat stains the worker red. Judgment is pictured as harvest work, because God gathers what men have sown and presses it out in full."},
{q:"What has he trodden alone in Isaiah 63:3?", options:["The winepress","The threshingfloor","The vineyard","The olive press"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 63:3",verse:"I have trodden the winepress alone; and of the people there was none with me: for I will tread them in mine anger, and trample them in my fury; and their blood shall be sprinkled upon my garments, and I will stain all my raiment.", insight:"Alone is the key word. No human helper stood with God in judgment, just as no helper stood with Christ in redemption. Both works are his alone."},
{q:"What was sprinkled upon his garments in Isaiah 63:3?", options:["Their blood","The new wine","The morning dew","The dust of battle"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 63:3",verse:"I have trodden the winepress alone; and of the people there was none with me: for I will tread them in mine anger, and trample them in my fury; and their blood shall be sprinkled upon my garments, and I will stain all my raiment.", insight:"Sin leaves a stain that only judgment or mercy can answer. The blood on the garments shows that rebellion carries a real and terrible cost."},
{q:"What is in his heart in Isaiah 63:4?", options:["The day of vengeance, and the year of his redeemed is come","The year of famine, and the day of mourning is come","The day of rest, and the year of jubilee is come","The year of planting, and the day of harvest is come"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 63:4",verse:"For the day of vengeance is in mine heart, and the year of my redeemed is come.", insight:"Vengeance and redemption arrive together. The same day that settles accounts with oppressors is the day that frees the oppressed."},
{q:"Why did his own arm bring salvation to him in Isaiah 63:5?", options:["Because there was none to help, and none to uphold","Because the angels refused to serve","Because the prophets had all fled","Because the nations had all surrendered"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 63:5",verse:"And I looked, and there was none to help; and I wondered that there was none to uphold: therefore mine own arm brought salvation unto me; and my fury, it upheld me.", insight:"When no one stood with God, he acted anyway. His salvation never depended on human cooperation, which is why it cannot be stopped by human failure."},
{q:"What will he bring down to the earth in Isaiah 63:6?", options:["Their strength","Their harvests","Their temples","Their ships"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 63:6",verse:"And I will tread down the people in mine anger, and make them drunk in my fury, and I will bring down their strength to the earth.", insight:"Human strength is the last idol to fall. God brings it low not out of cruelty but to show that every power besides his is borrowed and brief."},
{q:"What will the prophet mention in Isaiah 63:7?", options:["The lovingkindnesses of the LORD, and the praises of the LORD","The judgments of the LORD, and the curses of the law","The riches of the temple, and the vessels of gold","The victories of the kings, and the spoils of war"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 63:7",verse:"I will mention the lovingkindnesses of the LORD, and the praises of the LORD, according to all that the LORD hath bestowed on us, and the great goodness toward the house of Israel, which he hath bestowed on them according to his mercies, and according to the multitude of his lovingkindnesses.", insight:"After judgment comes deliberate remembering. Reciting God's mercies is how faith steadies itself when the present looks like wrath."},
{q:"What did God call the people in Isaiah 63:8?", options:["Children that will not lie, so he was their Saviour","Servants that will not tire, so he was their Master","Soldiers that will not flee, so he was their Captain","Builders that will not rest, so he was their Overseer"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 63:8",verse:"For he said, Surely they are my people, children that will not lie: so he was their Saviour.", insight:"God's expectation of honesty in his children is tender, not naive. He trusted them as a father trusts, and that trust is what made their rebellion so grievous."},
{q:"Which angel saved them in their affliction, according to Isaiah 63:9?", options:["The angel of his presence","The angel of the covenant","The angel of the whirlwind","The angel of the morning"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 63:9",verse:"In all their affliction he was afflicted, and the angel of his presence saved them: in his love and in his pity he redeemed them; and he bare them, and carried them all the days of old.", insight:"God does not send help from a distance; he comes in his own presence. His nearness in our affliction is itself the rescue."},
{q:"In Isaiah 63:18, who has trodden down thy sanctuary?", options:["Our adversaries","The merchants of Tyre","The kings of the north","The strangers from afar"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 63:18",verse:"The people of thy holiness have possessed it but a little while: our adversaries have trodden down thy sanctuary.", insight:"The prophet names the loss plainly instead of hiding it. Honest lament before God is the first step back toward him."},
{q:"What did they do to his holy Spirit in Isaiah 63:10?", options:["They rebelled, and vexed his holy Spirit","They obeyed, and pleased his holy Spirit","They forgot, and neglected his holy Spirit","They mocked, and denied his holy Spirit"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 63:10",verse:"But they rebelled, and vexed his holy Spirit: therefore he was turned to be their enemy, and he fought against them.", insight:"The Holy Spirit can be vexed, which means he is a person who feels. Grieving him is the quiet sin behind loud rebellion, and it turns a Father into an enemy."},
{q:"What did God then become to them in Isaiah 63:10?", options:["Their enemy, and he fought against them","Their stranger, and he departed from them","Their judge only, and he condemned them","Their master only, and he taxed them"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 63:10",verse:"But they rebelled, and vexed his holy Spirit: therefore he was turned to be their enemy, and he fought against them.", insight:"Persistent rebellion has a consequence no one wants to name: God himself stands opposed. This is why repentance is urgent, not optional."},
{q:"Whom did he remember in the days of old in Isaiah 63:11?", options:["Moses, and his people","Abraham, and his seed","David, and his house","Elijah, and the prophets"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 63:11",verse:"Then he remembered the days of old, Moses, and his people, saying, Where is he that brought them up out of the sea with the shepherd of his flock? where is he that put his holy Spirit within him?", insight:"Remembering is how God renews his work. He looks back to the exodus to find the pattern for the next deliverance, because his mercies are consistent."},
{q:"What question did they ask about the sea in Isaiah 63:11?", options:["Where is he that brought them up out of the sea with the shepherd of his flock?","Where is the gold that was taken out of Egypt?","Where are the chariots that drowned in the deep?","Where is the song that Miriam sang by the shore?"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 63:11",verse:"Then he remembered the days of old, Moses, and his people, saying, Where is he that brought them up out of the sea with the shepherd of his flock? where is he that put his holy Spirit within him?", insight:"In despair the people asked where God had gone. The right question in dark days is not where is he, but where is the God who already proved himself."},
{q:"By whose right hand did he lead them in Isaiah 63:12?", options:["Moses, with his glorious arm","Aaron, with his golden rod","Joshua, with his drawn sword","Caleb, with his faithful heart"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 63:12",verse:"That led them by the right hand of Moses with his glorious arm, dividing the water before them, to make himself an everlasting name?", insight:"God works through human hands he has chosen. Moses' arm was glorious only because God's arm was behind it, which is true of every servant God uses."},
{q:"What did he lead them through in Isaiah 63:13?", options:["The deep, as an horse in the wilderness, that they should not stumble","The desert, as a caravan in the night, that they should not thirst","The mountains, as an eagle in the air, that they should not fall","The valley, as a shepherd with his flock, that they should not stray"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 63:13",verse:"That led them through the deep, as an horse in the wilderness, that they should not stumble?", insight:"God led them through the deep without stumbling, which means the very place of their greatest danger became the road of their greatest safety."},
{q:"As a beast goes down into the valley, what did the Spirit of the LORD do in Isaiah 63:14?", options:["Caused him to rest","Taught him to run","Led him to water","Sheltered him from storm"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 63:14",verse:"As a beast goeth down into the valley, the Spirit of the LORD caused him to rest: so didst thou lead thy people, to make thyself a glorious name.", insight:"God's leading ends in rest, not exhaustion. The Spirit who guided Israel through the wilderness is the same Spirit who brings weary believers home."},
{q:"In Isaiah 63:17 the prophet pleads, \"Return for thy servants' sake.\" Who are the tribes named there?", options:["The tribes of thine inheritance","The tribes of the wilderness","The tribes of the captivity","The tribes of the seacoast"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 63:17",verse:"O LORD, why hast thou made us to err from thy ways, and hardened our heart from thy fear? Return for thy servants’ sake, the tribes of thine inheritance.", insight:"Pleading God's own inheritance is bold prayer. We ask him to act not because we deserve it but because we belong to him, and he keeps what is his."}
]},
{n:64, name:"The Potter and the Clay", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 64:8",verse:"But now, O LORD, thou art our father; we are the clay, and thou our potter; and we all are the work of thy hand."}, facts:["Did you know Isaiah 64 opens with one of the boldest prayers in Scripture: \"Oh that thou wouldest rend the heavens, that thou wouldest come down\"? The prophet asks God to tear the sky open and visit his people, a prayer finally answered in Christ.","Did you know \"filthy rags\" in Isaiah 64:6 is one of the most quoted phrases in all of Isaiah? Paul builds on the same idea when he writes that no one is justified by works of the law.","Did you know the potter and clay image appears in Jeremiah 18, Romans 9, and here? Each time it teaches the same lesson: the clay does not instruct the potter."], questions:[
{q:"What does the prophet wish God would rend in Isaiah 64:1?", options:["The heavens, that thou wouldest come down","The temple veil, that all might enter","The prison doors, that captives might flee","The scroll of judgment, that it might be erased"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 64:1",verse:"Oh that thou wouldest rend the heavens, that thou wouldest come down, that the mountains might flow down at thy presence,", insight:"True prayer is not polite; it is desperate for God's presence. The prophet would rather have God tear the heavens than keep a tidy distance."},
{q:"What might flow down at God's presence in Isaiah 64:1?", options:["The mountains","The rivers","The cities","The forests"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 64:1",verse:"Oh that thou wouldest rend the heavens, that thou wouldest come down, that the mountains might flow down at thy presence,", insight:"God's nearness melts what seems immovable. Mountains, the Bible's picture of permanence, flow down when he draws near, which means no obstacle survives his coming."},
{q:"In Isaiah 64:2, the fire causes the waters to do what?", options:["Boil","Freeze","Part","Dry up"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 64:2",verse:"As when the melting fire burneth, the fire causeth the waters to boil, to make thy name known to thine adversaries, that the nations may tremble at thy presence!", insight:"Fire and boiling water are pictures of irresistible power. When God acts to make his name known, nature itself cannot hold its shape."},
{q:"Why should God's name be made known to his adversaries in Isaiah 64:2?", options:["That the nations may tremble at thy presence","That the nations may pay him tribute","That the nations may learn his laws","That the nations may build him temples"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 64:2",verse:"As when the melting fire burneth, the fire causeth the waters to boil, to make thy name known to thine adversaries, that the nations may tremble at thy presence!", insight:"The goal of God's display is reverence, not applause. Trembling at his presence is the beginning of wisdom for nations that have defied him."},
{q:"What did God do when he did terrible things we looked not for?", options:["He came down, and the mountains flowed down at his presence","He sent angels, and the stars fell from heaven","He raised prophets, and the people turned in fear","He closed the heavens, and the rain ceased for years"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 64:3",verse:"When thou didst terrible things which we looked not for, thou camest down, the mountains flowed down at thy presence.", insight:"God has a history of surprising his people with deliverance. Remembering what he did unasked gives courage to ask for what seems impossible."},
{q:"What has no eye seen, according to Isaiah 64:4?", options:["What he hath prepared for him that waiteth for him","What he hath hidden in the deep places","What he hath written in the book of life","What he hath promised to the kings of earth"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 64:4",verse:"For since the beginning of the world men have not heard, nor perceived by the ear, neither hath the eye seen, O God, beside thee, what he hath prepared for him that waiteth for him.", insight:"God prepares things beyond imagination for those who wait. Waiting is not wasted time; it is the posture of those who expect wonders."},
{q:"Whom does God meet in Isaiah 64:5?", options:["Him that rejoiceth and worketh righteousness, those that remember thee in thy ways","Him that hideth and worketh in secret, those that fear the night","Him that boasteth and seeketh honor, those that love the praise of men","Him that murmureth and doubteth daily, those that forget thy covenant"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 64:5",verse:"Thou meetest him that rejoiceth and worketh righteousness, those that remember thee in thy ways: behold, thou art wroth; for we have sinned: in those is continuance, and we shall be saved.", insight:"God meets those who remember him in their daily walk. Righteousness is not a single grand act but a remembered God in ordinary ways."},
{q:"All our righteousnesses are as what in Isaiah 64:6?", options:["Filthy rags","Broken cisterns","Withered grass","Empty vessels"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 64:6",verse:"But we are all as an unclean thing, and all our righteousnesses are as filthy rags; and we all do fade as a leaf; and our iniquities, like the wind, have taken us away.", insight:"Our best deeds, offered as payment to God, are stained cloth. This verse kills self righteousness so that grace can clothe us instead."},
{q:"Why has God hid his face from us in Isaiah 64:7?", options:["Because of our iniquities","Because of our poverty","Because of our enemies","Because of our silence"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 64:7",verse:"And there is none that calleth upon thy name, that stirreth up himself to take hold of thee: for thou hast hid thy face from us, and hast consumed us, because of our iniquities.", insight:"Sin, not God's absence, is the barrier. His face is hidden because iniquity stands between, and confession is what removes it."},
{q:"Our iniquities, like the wind, have done what in Isaiah 64:6?", options:["Taken us away","Broken our bones","Darkened our eyes","Stopped our ears"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 64:6",verse:"But we are all as an unclean thing, and all our righteousnesses are as filthy rags; and we all do fade as a leaf; and our iniquities, like the wind, have taken us away.", insight:"Sin is pictured as a wind that carries us off. We do not merely commit iniquities; they transport us away from God, which is why we need to be carried back."},
{q:"What is there none of in Isaiah 64:7?", options:["None that calleth upon thy name, that stirreth up himself to take hold of thee","None that keepeth the Sabbath, that honoreth the holy day","None that bringeth an offering, that layeth it on the altar","None that singeth thy praise, that lifteth up a song"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 64:7",verse:"And there is none that calleth upon thy name, that stirreth up himself to take hold of thee: for thou hast hid thy face from us, and hast consumed us, because of our iniquities.", insight:"The deepest crisis in this chapter is not sin but prayerlessness. When no one calls on God's name, the people have stopped reaching for their only rescue."},
{q:"We are the clay, and who is the potter in Isaiah 64:8?", options:["Thou, O LORD, our father","Moses, the servant of God","Aaron, the high priest","David, the king of Israel"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 64:8",verse:"But now, O LORD, thou art our father; we are the clay, and thou our potter; and we all are the work of thy hand.", insight:"Calling God Father and Potter in one breath holds two truths together: he shapes us with authority and loves us with tenderness."},
{q:"We are all the work of whose hand in Isaiah 64:8?", options:["Thy hand","Our own hand","The craftsmen's hand","The builders' hand"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 64:8",verse:"But now, O LORD, thou art our father; we are the clay, and thou our potter; and we all are the work of thy hand.", insight:"The clay does not explain itself to the potter. Accepting that we are his workmanship frees us from both pride in our making and despair over our flaws."},
{q:"What does the prophet beg God not to remember forever in Isaiah 64:9?", options:["Iniquity","The famine","The exile","The plunder"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 64:9",verse:"Be not wroth very sore, O LORD, neither remember iniquity for ever: behold, see, we beseech thee, we are all thy people.", insight:"The prayer is not that God pretend sin never happened but that he not hold it forever. Mercy asks for an end to the remembering, and God in Christ grants it."},
{q:"What has Zion become in Isaiah 64:10?", options:["A wilderness","A garden","A marketplace","A fortress"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 64:10",verse:"Thy holy cities are a wilderness, Zion is a wilderness, Jerusalem a desolation.", insight:"Zion, meant to be the joy of the earth, lies waste. The verse names the ruin plainly, because honest grief is the doorway to honest prayer."},
{q:"What has Jerusalem become in Isaiah 64:10?", options:["A desolation","A celebration","A refuge","A stronghold"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 64:10",verse:"Thy holy cities are a wilderness, Zion is a wilderness, Jerusalem a desolation.", insight:"Jerusalem's desolation is the price of forsaking God. The city that should have shown his glory became the exhibit of what happens without him."},
{q:"What happened to the holy and beautiful house in Isaiah 64:11?", options:["It is burned up with fire","It is filled with strangers","It is covered with gold","It is shut up forever"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 64:11",verse:"Our holy and our beautiful house, where our fathers praised thee, is burned up with fire: and all our pleasant things are laid waste.", insight:"Even the temple can burn. When the house of worship is lost, the prophet does not defend the building; he pleads with the God who outlives it."},
{q:"Where did the fathers praise God in Isaiah 64:11?", options:["In the holy and beautiful house","In the tents of the wilderness","On the tops of the mountains","In the courts of the kings"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 64:11",verse:"Our holy and our beautiful house, where our fathers praised thee, is burned up with fire: and all our pleasant things are laid waste.", insight:"The memory of fathers praising God in that house makes the ashes heavier. Faith remembers what worship used to be and asks God to restore it."},
{q:"What does the prophet ask God not to hold in Isaiah 64:12?", options:["His peace","His anger","His hand","His word"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 64:12",verse:"Wilt thou refrain thyself for these things, O LORD? wilt thou hold thy peace, and afflict us very sore?", insight:"The chapter ends with a question, not an answer. Sometimes the most faithful prayer is to ask God if he will really stay silent, and then wait for him to speak."},
{q:"In Isaiah 64:12 the prophet asks if God will afflict whom very sore?", options:["Us, his people","The nations round about","The kings of the earth","The armies of the north"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 64:12",verse:"Wilt thou refrain thyself for these things, O LORD? wilt thou hold thy peace, and afflict us very sore?", insight:"Us is the honest word. The prophet includes himself in the afflicted, because intercession never prays from a safe distance."}
]},
{n:65, name:"New Heavens and a New Earth", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 65:17",verse:"For, behold, I create new heavens and a new earth: and the former shall not be remembered, nor come into mind."}, facts:["Did you know Isaiah 65:17 is the first promise of \"new heavens and a new earth\" in the Bible? Peter and John pick it up in 2 Peter 3 and Revelation 21, where God finally makes all things new.","Did you know \"before they call, I will answer\" (Isaiah 65:24) is one of Scripture's sweetest promises about prayer? God hears the prayer before the lips have finished forming it.","Did you know the wolf and the lamb feeding together (Isaiah 65:25) echoes Isaiah 11? The same peace among animals appears in both visions of God's restored creation."], questions:[
{q:"To whom did God say, \"Behold me, behold me,\" in Isaiah 65:1?", options:["Unto a nation that was not called by my name","Unto the priests of the house of Levi","Unto the elders of the city of David","Unto the prophets of the house of Israel"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 65:1",verse:"I am sought of them that asked not for me; I am found of them that sought me not: I said, Behold me, behold me, unto a nation that was not called by my name.", insight:"God offers himself to those who were not even looking. Grace goes first to the unexpected, which means no one is too far off to be found."},
{q:"To what people did God spread out his hands all the day in Isaiah 65:2?", options:["A rebellious people, which walk after their own thoughts","A faithful people, which keep all his statutes","A weary people, which rest in his promises","A joyful people, which sing in his courts"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 65:2",verse:"I have spread out my hands all the day unto a rebellious people, which walketh in a way that was not good, after their own thoughts;", insight:"God's hands are spread all the day, which is a picture of patient pleading. Rebellion is answered first with an open embrace, not a closed fist."},
{q:"What did the rebellious people sacrifice in, according to Isaiah 65:3?", options:["Gardens, burning incense upon altars of brick","Temples, burning incense upon altars of gold","Caves, burning incense upon altars of stone","Fields, burning incense upon altars of wood"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 65:3",verse:"A people that provoketh me to anger continually to my face; that sacrificeth in gardens, and burneth incense upon altars of brick;", insight:"They built their own altars in their own gardens. Idolatry always starts with worship on our terms, in places we chose instead of the place God chose."},
{q:"Where do they remain and lodge in Isaiah 65:4?", options:["Among the graves, and in the monuments","Among the merchants, and in the markets","Among the soldiers, and in the barracks","Among the shepherds, and in the pastures"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 65:4",verse:"Which remain among the graves, and lodge in the monuments, which eat swine’s flesh, and broth of abominable things is in their vessels;", insight:"They made their home among the dead. Sin's practices always lead to death's neighborhood, even when they feel like freedom at first."},
{q:"What abominable food is in their vessels in Isaiah 65:4?", options:["Swine's flesh, and broth of abominable things","Bread of idols, and wine of sorceries","Meat of strangled beasts, and blood of sacrifices","Fish of the sea, and herbs of the field"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 65:4",verse:"Which remain among the graves, and lodge in the monuments, which eat swine’s flesh, and broth of abominable things is in their vessels;", insight:"The unclean food was a deliberate breaking of God's law. Small defiances reveal a heart that has decided it knows better than God."},
{q:"What proud words do they say in Isaiah 65:5?", options:["Stand by thyself, come not near to me; for I am holier than thou","Go thy way, I need no counsel; for I am wiser than thou","Keep thy distance, I owe thee nothing; for I am richer than thou","Hold thy peace, I fear no judgment; for I am stronger than thou"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 65:5",verse:"Which say, Stand by thyself, come not near to me; for I am holier than thou. These are a smoke in my nose, a fire that burneth all the day.", insight:"I am holier than thou is religion's ugliest sentence. Pride that dresses as piety is smoke in God's nose, because it claims a purity only he can give."},
{q:"What is written before God in Isaiah 65:6?", options:["I will not keep silence, but will recompense into their bosom","I will forget their sins, and remember them no more","I will send them prophets, and they shall be warned","I will shut the heavens, and there shall be no rain"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 65:6",verse:"Behold, it is written before me: I will not keep silence, but will recompense, even recompense into their bosom,", insight:"God keeps a record, which is both warning and comfort. Nothing unjust is forgotten by him, and nothing faithful is overlooked either."},
{q:"Where did they burn incense and blaspheme God in Isaiah 65:7?", options:["Upon the mountains, and upon the hills","In the valleys, and beside the rivers","In the cities, and within the gates","In the deserts, and under the trees"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 65:7",verse:"Your iniquities, and the iniquities of your fathers together, saith the LORD, which have burned incense upon the mountains, and blasphemed me upon the hills: therefore will I measure their former work into their bosom.", insight:"High places were the rival altars of idolatry. Worship offered anywhere but where God commanded always ends in blasphemy, however sincere it feels."},
{q:"Why should the cluster not be destroyed in Isaiah 65:8?", options:["For a blessing is in it","For the harvest is great","For the season is short","For the workers are few"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 65:8",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, As the new wine is found in the cluster, and one saith, Destroy it not; for a blessing is in it: so will I do for my servants’ sakes, that I may not destroy them all.", insight:"God spares the whole cluster for the blessing hidden inside. His judgments always leave a remnant, because mercy looks for a reason to preserve."},
{q:"Out of whom will God bring forth a seed in Isaiah 65:9?", options:["Out of Jacob, and out of Judah an inheritor of my mountains","Out of Joseph, and out of Ephraim an inheritor of my valleys","Out of Benjamin, and out of Dan an inheritor of my rivers","Out of Levi, and out of Aaron an inheritor of my altar"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 65:9",verse:"And I will bring forth a seed out of Jacob, and out of Judah an inheritor of my mountains: and mine elect shall inherit it, and my servants shall dwell there.", insight:"The seed comes from Jacob, the schemer turned Israel. God's promises run through flawed families, which means your family line is no barrier to his plan."},
{q:"What shall Sharon be in Isaiah 65:10?", options:["A fold of flocks","A field of wheat","A garden of spices","A forest of cedars"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 65:10",verse:"And Sharon shall be a fold of flocks, and the valley of Achor a place for the herds to lie down in, for my people that have sought me.", insight:"Sharon, a plain known for beauty, becomes pasture for God's flock. Restoration turns lovely places into useful ones, where his people are fed and kept."},
{q:"What did they prepare in Isaiah 65:11?", options:["A table for that troop, and the drink offering unto that number","An altar for the LORD, and the sacrifice of praise","A feast for the poor, and the cup of blessing","A covenant with death, and an agreement with hell"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 65:11",verse:"But ye are they that forsake the LORD, that forget my holy mountain, that prepare a table for that troop, and that furnish the drink offering unto that number.", insight:"They set a table for idols while forgetting God's mountain. Whatever we feast without God becomes a feast against him, because worship always has an object."},
{q:"Why will God number them to the sword in Isaiah 65:12?", options:["Because when he called, they did not answer; when he spoke, they did not hear","Because when he sent rain, they did not gather it","Because when he gave harvest, they did not thank him","Because when he built the temple, they did not enter it"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 65:12",verse:"Therefore will I number you to the sword, and ye shall all bow down to the slaughter: because when I called, ye did not answer; when I spake, ye did not hear; but did evil before mine eyes, and did choose that wherein I delighted not.", insight:"Judgment answers a refused invitation. God called and spoke first; the sword comes only after every word was ignored, which makes the verdict just."},
{q:"My servants shall eat, but you shall be what in Isaiah 65:13?", options:["Hungry","Weary","Silent","Blind"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 65:13",verse:"Therefore thus saith the Lord GOD, Behold, my servants shall eat, but ye shall be hungry: behold, my servants shall drink, but ye shall be thirsty: behold, my servants shall rejoice, but ye shall be ashamed:", insight:"God draws a sharp line between his servants and those who refused him. The same God feeds one table and withholds from another, and the difference is obedience."},
{q:"My servants shall sing for joy of heart, but you shall do what in Isaiah 65:14?", options:["Cry for sorrow of heart, and howl for vexation of spirit","Laugh for scorn of heart, and mock with bitterness of soul","Sleep for weariness of heart, and dream of vanity","Tremble for fear of heart, and hide in the darkness"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 65:14",verse:"Behold, my servants shall sing for joy of heart, but ye shall cry for sorrow of heart, and shall howl for vexation of spirit.", insight:"Joy and sorrow flow from the same source: our response to God. The singing and the howling are both honest; only one of them lasts."},
{q:"What will the Lord GOD call his servants in Isaiah 65:15?", options:["By another name","By the name of Jacob","By the name of Israel","By the name of Judah"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 65:15",verse:"And ye shall leave your name for a curse unto my chosen: for the Lord GOD shall slay thee, and call his servants by another name:", insight:"A new name means a new identity. Those who were known by their fathers' failures will be known by God's own choosing."},
{q:"What is forgotten and hid from God's eyes in Isaiah 65:16?", options:["The former troubles","The former sins","The former idols","The former sorrows"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 65:16",verse:"That he who blesseth himself in the earth shall bless himself in the God of truth; and he that sweareth in the earth shall swear by the God of truth; because the former troubles are forgotten, and because they are hid from mine eyes.", insight:"God forgets on purpose. The former troubles are hid from his eyes, which means his people can stop rehearsing what he has already buried."},
{q:"What will happen before they call in Isaiah 65:24?", options:["I will answer","I will send rain","I will build the city","I will silence the enemy"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 65:24",verse:"And it shall come to pass, that before they call, I will answer; and while they are yet speaking, I will hear.", insight:"God answers prayer before it is fully spoken. His attentiveness runs ahead of our asking, which means prayer is entering a conversation he already started."},
{q:"What shall be no more heard in Jerusalem in Isaiah 65:19?", options:["The voice of weeping, nor the voice of crying","The voice of singing, nor the voice of praise","The voice of trading, nor the voice of buying","The voice of building, nor the voice of planting"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 65:19",verse:"And I will rejoice in Jerusalem, and joy in my people: and the voice of weeping shall be no more heard in her, nor the voice of crying.", insight:"God's own joy in his people silences their tears. When he rejoices over Jerusalem, weeping loses its address, because its cause is gone."},
{q:"In Isaiah 65:25, the wolf and the lamb shall do what?", options:["Feed together, and the lion shall eat straw like the bullock","Run together, and the lion shall sleep beside the kid","Drink together, and the bear shall graze with the ox","Rest together, and the serpent shall lose its venom"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 65:25",verse:"The wolf and the lamb shall feed together, and the lion shall eat straw like the bullock: and dust shall be the serpent’s meat. They shall not hurt nor destroy in all my holy mountain, saith the LORD.", insight:"Peace in God's holy mountain reaches even the animals. If wolves and lambs can feed together, then no enmity among his people is beyond his healing."}
]}
]);
